00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.119 align:start position:0%
 
I'm joined today by retired Seattle  police chief norm Stamper who spent 34  years in law enforcement and now  promotes common-sense solutions to  criminal justice policing and drug  policy issues as a representative of the  law enforcement Action Partnership it's  so great to speak to you norm I think  just to start with to begin with I'd  love to get your general sense of the  direction that Jeff sessions is taking  the Justice Department with regard to  the war on drugs and enforcement of drug  policy are you pleased with what you see  or are you concerned well I'm absolutely  not pleased I think what Jeff Sessions  and the entire confidence tration is  advocating and in fact acting upon is a  reversal of very hard-fought gains in  criminal justice system reform over the  last 10 15 years and when we look at for  example just to pick one issue to dig  into a little more specifically when we  look at establishing prison sentences  for drug crimes talk to us about in your  experience in Seattle what did you see  if anything in terms of the correlation  between harsher sentences for drug  crimes and preventing violent drug crime  you know I think that distinction is  critical sessions and others don't seem  to want to make that distinction but  it's very important to do so what I  observed in Seattle and my 28 years in  the San Diego Police Department before  that was that the drug war itself is  fundamentally flawed it is most  disastrous a social or public policy in  my experience in this country since  slavery I think what we're seeing is a  return to some draconian penalties that  will do nothing to enhance Public Safety  or neighborhood health it seems that if  we sort of divide drug crimes into  violent and nonviolent from what I've  read if you apply harsh sentences  to those who commit nonviolent drug  related crimes you actually risk by the  environment that you expose these  individuals to I don't know if  radicalizing is the right word but maybe  predisposing them to get involved in  more serious crime and then on the  violent drug crime part it doesn't  really appear that the harsh sentences  sort of serve as a deterrent so I'm just  not sure in what way we benefit from  harsh drug sentences you know I'll take  the latter part of that question first  okay I just don't think it's reasonable  for us to think that the heads of drug  cartels or major traffickers or hand to  hand dealers in neighborhoods and  communities are studying sentencing  patterns and in informing their choices  to engage in that illicit behavior what  they're doing is making massive amounts  of money and the product of an illicit  commerce basically is violence that's  inevitable so what we see really is the  fundamental flaw in that policy  producing the crime violence and other  unfortunate social conditions that we  see in our neighborhoods but you're  absolutely right one of the best ways to  educate in the ways of accelerating  advancing criminal behavior those  engaged in nonviolent offenses is to put  them in prison where they're going to  learn a ton about how to commit criminal  acts how to disable and disarm police  officers how I've seen this firsthand in  in San Diego as I visited prisons  throughout the state of California a few  years back and one of the things that  that I think is just vital for us to  understand is that the organizing  principle behind this country's drug war  is prohibition and we know from  literally centuries of experience that  prohibition doesn't work that it can't  work when Nixon famously proclaimed  drugs public enemy number one and  declared all-out war on them it was  really declaring war on his own people  and disproportionately young people poor  people  people of color the very people in  neighborhoods across this country who  have had the most difficult time of  their local police so he put domestic  peacekeepers on the front line of a drug  war and of course you don't fight a war  without enemies you don't fight a war  for that matter without propaganda the  last ten years or so we have become much  smarter about the drug war and we have  said that those who are engaged in  nonviolent offenses are are typically in  the in the midst of a public health  issue  and and certainly with the opioid crisis  of a public health crisis and with we  were to treat drug use drugs certainly  drug abuse as a medical problem the  medical problem that it is we'd save a  ton of money and we would make our  communities safer and and healthier yeah  I mean it seems like it at all levels so  many of the interventions that have been  attempted by under the umbrella of I  guess what we would call the war on  drugs have just failed to sort of impact  the right places or the right people  what one example I recently read this  book in our economics by Tom Wainwright  which is super interesting and it talks  about the cocaine trade and it points  out I don't remember the exact numbers  but I think in the United States the  street-level value of a kilo of cocaine  was roughly a hundred or 120 thousand  dollars I mean does that sound roughly  right to you yes it does look at things  we know that it fluctuates as a function  of supply and demand yeah so one of the  techniques that has been applied in  Colombia where coca leaf has grown is to  basically burn or destroy the coca leaf  plantations and the amount of coca leaf  that you need to end up with that  hundred thousand dollar kilo of refined  cocaine is like a couple hundred dollars  and what the book points out is that  even if you burn half of the supply of  coca leaf in Colombia effectively  doubling the source materials cost from  say two hundred to four hundred dollars  it basically has no impact at the street  level right I mean it's a hundred and  twenty  thousand dollar kilo or a hundred and  twenty thousand four hundred dollar kilo  and you're really affecting farmers in  Colombia who are not really the source  of the cocaine problem is that sort of  representative of how the war on drugs  has failed it sort of impacting the  wrong people it is and we need to bear  in mind you talk about Colombia the  Middle East is a hotbed of opium  production as well and you can be sure  that the farmers who are growing and  harvesting that crop are not being  compensated the Taliban is terrorists to  attack this country are in many cases  financing their terrorism through in  effect u.s. drug policy so if we were to  get smart we would legalize all drugs  however radical that may sound we would  text and regulate those drugs so you  cannot regulate illicit commerce but if  we were to create a model fundamentally  different from the one that has guided  us and misguided us for so many decades  we would in fact begin to see a major  reduction in public air street street  dealing we would see an increase in  property values and disadvantaged  communities we would see fewer families  fractured we would see enormous  improvements in our crime rates sessions  likes to talk about how crime has  skyrocketed in the last year or two that  corresponds directly with the opioid  crisis which began frankly in doctors  offices and medicine chests right and  his mouth spilled out onto the streets  and the problem that exists today exists  under current US drug policy drugs are  more readily available today at lower  prices and higher levels of potency then  at any time since Nixon declared that  war on drugs yeah it seems if you wanted  to go with the first approach which is  sort of try to impact some of the  individuals involved in the trade to  stick with the cocaine example you don't  go with the coca leaf growers and  you don't go with the street-level  dealers you go with sort of the maybe  the cartel or the place in the middle  where the most cost or is added to the  price of cocaine but you're actually  talking about what maybe makes the most  sense which is you just don't treat it  as a criminal issue whatsoever you treat  it as an issue of Public Health and  would you not I mean this is sort of  there's disagreement over this but is  your sense norm that when you do  legalize you don't remove all of the  power from cartels but you do remove a  lot of it you remove their profits if  you were to legalize across the board  all drugs you would almost overnight  eliminate that particular problem the  carnet cartels are populated by evil and  greedy people and I have no doubt that  they would funnel their activities and  their energy and their tactics into  other areas like human trafficking for  example or gun-running or some other  illicit form of Commerce but we could  dramatically improve the health of  America's cities if we were in fact to  remove that cartel influence from  essentially drug Commerce and to  regulate it tax it which then enables us  to control it and by the way in offering  an agenda of legalization the law  enforcement Action Partnership of which  I am a proud member and my own  experience over the last 50 years in and  out of law enforcement suggests that you  concentrate on the behavior if you've  got somebody in a neighborhood who is  furnishing to kids you pop that  individual you arrest that individual  and and you apply a rigorous method and  policies of prosecution charging  hopefully with conviction and you make  those people who engage in that behavior  pay a price we've been speaking with  retired Seattle police chief norm  Stamper norm thanks so much for talking  to us today it's my pleasure thanks for  having me

